{#- Model card: BG/NBD and Gamma-Gamma lifetime value, and the allowance built on it. -#}
{% import '_macros.j2' as m %}
{% set card = 'clv-bgnbd-gamma-gamma' %}
{% set title = 'BG/NBD and Gamma-Gamma lifetime value' %}
{% set infeasible = raw('budget.allowance_infeasible') %}
{% set infeasible_key = m.key_of(infeasible) %}
{% set binds = [] %}
{% for key, name in m.channels if raw('budget.bound.' ~ key) == 'allowance' %}{% set _ = binds.append(name) %}{% endfor %}
{% set over = raw('clv.holdout.purchases_predicted_over_actual') > 0 %}
{% set intended %}
BG/NBD predicts how many purchases a customer will make and whether they are still a customer;
Gamma-Gamma predicts how much they spend when they buy. Together they give each customer's
lifetime value over the next {{ v('policy.clv_horizon_months') }} months, and from it the acquisition
cost allowance: the most a channel may spend to acquire a customer, which the budget optimizer and
the API apply. The transactions are a UK online gift retailer's, largely wholesale customers, from
2009 to 2011, and Alderquist's market is simulated, so the allowance is a demonstration of the
mechanism, not a number for Alderquist. It is not meant for valuing Alderquist's customers, for
one customer's value (the holdout grades deciles and totals, not individuals), for customers the
model has not seen, or for pricing an acquisition before it is refit on the brand's own purchase
history.
{% endset %}
{% set data %}
Online Retail II from the UCI Machine Learning Repository: {{ v('data.retail.raw_rows') }} transaction
lines from {{ v('data.retail.first_date') }} to {{ v('data.retail.last_date') }}. After the sheet overlap
is dropped and credit notes, lines without a customer id and non positive adjustments are removed
(`data/PROVENANCE.md` has each count), {{ v('data.retail.kept_rows') }} lines remain, from
{{ v('data.retail.customers') }} customers over {{ v('data.retail.purchases') }} customer days.
{{ label('data.retail.kept_rows') }}

The models are fit on the {{ v('clv.customers') }} customers who bought by {{ v('clv.calibration_end') }},
{{ v('clv.customers_repeat') }} of them repeat buyers, and judged on what those customers did from
{{ v('clv.holdout_start') }} to {{ v('clv.holdout_end') }}. The {{ v('clv.customers_new_in_holdout') }}
customers whose first purchase falls in the holdout are counted but not predicted, because the
models describe customers they have seen. The committed frame holds a pseudonymous customer id, a
day, revenue and lines, nothing else. Amounts are the retailer's own, in pounds sterling, printed
in the repository's dollar format. {{ label('clv.holdout_by_decile') }}
{% endset %}
{% set fit %}
Both models are written in the repository (`marginal_clv`) and fit by maximum likelihood on the
calibration window, with BFGS on the log parameters and the analytic gradient. BG/NBD took
{{ v('clv.bgnbd.evaluations') }} evaluations and {{ 'converged' if raw('clv.bgnbd.converged') == 'yes' else 'did not converge' }},
judged by the largest gradient component at the answer and not by the optimizer's flag alone. On this file its
likelihood has a long, nearly flat ridge along which a and b grow together, so the tolerance is
tight on purpose. Gamma-Gamma uses the repeat purchases of repeat customers only, and assumes that
spend per purchase does not depend on how often a customer buys; the correlation between the two
among repeat customers is {{ v('clv.gg.correlation') }}, which does not rule the model out.

Lifetime value is the discounted expected revenue over {{ v('policy.clv_horizon_months') }} months at an
annual rate of {{ v('policy.clv_discount_rate_annual') }}, computed at the calibration end from the
models the holdout judged, not refit on the whole file. The allowance is lifetime value times the
{{ v('policy.contribution_margin') }} contribution margin times a payback share of
{{ v('policy.clv_payback_share') }}: per purchase frequency segment, overall, and per channel as a mix
weighted mean of the segment allowances. The channel mixes are stated assumptions in
`marginal_clv.value`, because the retail file records no channel. The budget optimizer turns a
channel's incremental revenue into customers at {{ v('clv.revenue_per_acquisition') }} per acquisition,
the mean revenue on a customer's first purchase day, and holds spend per customer under the
channel's allowance.

As a cross check, PyMC-Marketing fits the same two models to the same frame by maximum a posteriori
under flat priors.
{% endset %}
{% set metrics %}
The parameters, beside PyMC-Marketing's. The largest relative difference across them is
{{ v('clv.crosscheck.max_relative_difference') }}.

{{ table('clv.parameters') }}

{{ label('clv.parameters') }}

{% call m.holds(over and raw('clv.holdout.top_decile_purchases_predicted_over_actual') < raw('clv.holdout.purchases_predicted_over_actual'), 'that BG/NBD over predicts the holdout and the top decile is closer') %}
**The holdout.** The model expected {{ v('clv.holdout.predicted_purchases_total') }} purchases in the
holdout and the customers made {{ v('clv.holdout.actual_purchases_total') }}, a difference of
{{ v('clv.holdout.purchases_predicted_over_actual') }}; predicted revenue is {{ v('clv.holdout.predicted_revenue_total') }}
against {{ v('clv.holdout.actual_revenue_total') }}, a difference of {{ v('clv.holdout.revenue_predicted_over_actual') }}.
The mean absolute error per customer is {{ v('clv.holdout.mae_purchases') }} purchases and
{{ v('clv.holdout.mae_revenue') }} of revenue. The over prediction comes from the dropout parameters: the
fitted chance that a customer stops buying after a purchase is {{ v('clv.bgnbd.dropout_after_purchase') }},
close to zero, so the model treats nearly every customer who went quiet as still active and keeps
predicting purchases for them. The tenth decile, the customers who bought most often in the
calibration window, is much closer: {{ v('clv.holdout.top_decile_purchases_predicted_over_actual') }}.
{% endcall %}

