{% endset %}
{% set contract %}
{% call m.line('trained', 'partly') %}
Fit in the pipeline (`make clv`) on the calibration window to {{ v('clv.calibration_end') }} and judged against what the same customers did in the holdout, with PyMC-Marketing as a cross check; no simpler benchmark, such as last year's purchases carried forward, was fit beside it.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('timed', 'met') %}
The calibration window ends {{ v('clv.calibration_end') }} and the holdout runs from {{ v('clv.holdout_start') }} to {{ v('clv.holdout_end') }}; a test checks the two never overlap, and lifetime value is computed at the calibration end from the models the holdout judged.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('calibrated', 'partly') %}
The holdout by decile checks predicted purchases and revenue against actual, and `results/clv/calibration_plot.parquet` makes the same check in bins of predicted purchases; the check shows an over prediction of {{ v('clv.holdout.purchases_predicted_over_actual') }}, and no interval is published for lifetime value or the allowance.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('useful', 'met') %}
It sets the acquisition cost allowance the plan and the API apply: lifetime value times the {{ v('policy.contribution_margin') }} margin times a payback share of {{ v('policy.clv_payback_share') }}, {{ v('clv.allowance.overall') }} per customer overall.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('fair', 'partly') %}
The frame holds a pseudonymous id, a day, revenue and lines, with no demographic field to check; the allowance differs by purchase frequency segment by design, and the channel mixes that weight the segments are stated, not measured.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('gated', 'partly') %}
A fit whose maximum sits on the edge of the parameter space is not called converged (a test checks this), and the stage refuses an empty or overlapping window; a fit that does not converge is published with its flag rather than stopped.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('served', 'partly') %}
The model is not served, its allowance is: the API reads `results/clv/allowance.json` and applies it in `/v1/optimize` and `/v1/plans` unless a request turns it off.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('integrated', 'met') %}
The budget stage and the API apply the allowance, the site's Budget page shows where it binds, and the quarterly review's lifetime value section quotes the holdout and the allowance.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('monitored', 'not built') %}
There is no refit on new purchases and no check of the holdout error over time; the weekly refresh is a stretch goal.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('documented', 'met') %}
This card and `data/PROVENANCE.md`, rendered from the manifest by the claim gate.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('bounded', 'met') %}
The statement closes this card, and the intended use above says it plainly: the customers are a UK gift retailer's, the market they are applied to is simulated, and the allowance demonstrates the mechanism.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('validated', 'met') %}
Judged on the holdout year of the same customers, by decile of calibration frequency, and cross checked against PyMC-Marketing, never on the window it was fit on.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('explainable at the decision', 'met') %}
A reader sees the holdout by decile, the parameters beside PyMC-Marketing's, the lifetime value distribution, the allowance by segment and by channel, and in the plan the channels where it binds.
{% endcall %}
{% endset %}
{% set limitations %}
{% if over %}
It over predicts: its holdout purchases are {{ v('clv.holdout.purchases_predicted_over_actual') }} against
what the customers bought, so every lifetime value and every allowance built on it leans
optimistic. A model in which customers can drop out between purchases, such as Pareto/NBD, is the
next thing to try.

{% endif %}
The customers are a UK online gift retailer's, largely wholesale buyers, whose purchase rhythm and
basket sizes are not those of a direct to consumer home goods brand. The amounts are pounds
sterling, and the plan applies them to Alderquist's dollars one for one.

The channel allowances rest on segment mixes that are stated, not measured, and move one for one
with them. A segment is a customer's purchase frequency in the calibration window, which also
reflects how long they have been a customer; reading it as the kind of customer a channel acquires
is the simplification the mixes rest on.

No interval is published for lifetime value or the allowance.
{% endset %}
